<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ngazargamu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ngazargamu was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a> phase of the Kanem-Bornu Empire, located west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a> in present-day north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. It replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/njimi">Njimi</a> after the dynasty moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kanem">Kanem</a> to Bornu. The city served as a major commercial and administrative centre for several centuries.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ngazargamu, also written Gazargamu, Gazargamo and Birni Gazargamo, stood in the fork of the Komadugu Gana and the Komadugu Yobe rivers, near present-day Geidam, about 150 km west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a> in Yobe State. It lay at the southern end of the trans-Saharan trade routes, closer to the rising markets of Hausaland than earlier royal seats, and in the middle of a salt-producing district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a>, salt being a profitable article of trade. At its height the city covered about 1,500 hectares; estimates of its population range from 60,000 to 250,000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 14th century the Bilala took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/njimi">Njimi</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayfawa-dynasty">Sayfawa dynasty</a> lost its old heartland east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a>. For a time the empire had no fixed capital and suffered civil wars between branches of the dynasty, until Ali I Gaji gathered power in the later 15th century and made Ngazargamu his seat. By the 17th century caravans came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>, Agades and Gao, and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kordofan">Kordofan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, bringing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, carpets, weapons and books, paper and glass <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, and gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kola-nut">kola nuts</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti</a>. A description by Mallam Salih ibn Ishaku, translated by H. R. Palmer in the 1920s, speaks of six hundred and sixty roads and four Friday mosques.</p><p>In 1808 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a>, fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem-Bornu</a> in the Fula jihads, captured and destroyed the city, and Mai Ahmad Alimi fled and abdicated in favour of his son Dunama IX Lefiami. With the help of Muhammad al-Amin al-Kanemi, Dunama drove the Fulani back and settled in the ruins, but he left again in 1809 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-zaki">Ibrahim Zaki</a> approached. He never returned, and in the early 1810s his uncle Muhammad IX Ngileruma chose Kafela as the new capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The roughly circular earth rampart on which the city stood is still about 7 metres high, with traces of a ditch outside it and of a wall that once ran round a circuit some two kilometres across, entered by five gates. Inside are ruins of red brick buildings, rare in pre-modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>: a large central complex, very likely the palace of the mai, and smaller structures probably used by leading figures. In 2015 the government of Yobe State formed a committee to conserve the ruins and promote the site.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ngazargamu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>There Mai Ali Gaji founded a new capital at <b>Ngazargamu</b> around 1470, and Kanem became Kanem–Bornu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem-Bornu Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1808 he joined other Fulani leaders in capturing the Bornu capital, Birni <b>Ngazargamu</b>, and in about 1807-1809 he founded the town of Katagum as the seat of his emirate.</blockquote><a href="/state/katagum-emirate">Katagum Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
